# Translation of Odoo Server.
# This file contains the translation of the following modules:
# * biz_week_grid
#
# W7 — the markers this file is written to, and why each one is here.
#
#   1. EVERY user-facing string in the module, or none of them. A component
#      that is half translated is worse than one that is not: the Vietnamese
#      officer reads "Chỉ các dòng có giờ" beside "Save 3" and learns that the
#      language setting is unreliable, so they stop trusting the ones that ARE
#      translated.
#   2. The strings live in three places and only two of them are obvious. The
#      component's JS (`_t(...)`) and the templates' TEXT NODES and translatable
#      attributes are extracted automatically. A literal inside a `t-att`
#      EXPRESSION (`t-esc="x or 'Locked'"`, `t-attf-title="Open {{ … }}"`) is
#      NOT — it reads perfectly in English and is simply absent from every .po,
#      forever, with nothing to report it. P7 moved all six of those into
#      `_t`-wrapped getters; that is why entries like "Locked", "Budget" and
#      "No rows to show" exist here at all.
#   3. A sentence is ONE entry. "3 cells edited · 4.5 h overtime drafted" is
#      built by `trayLabel` rather than assembled from `<t>` fragments in the
#      template, because a translator cannot reorder fragments and Vietnamese
#      does not put the number where English does.
#   4. Placeholders keep their names and their count. `%(cells)s` / `%(hours)s`
#      are named precisely so the Vietnamese word order can differ from the
#      English one; `msgfmt --check-format` is what proves none was dropped.
#   5. Keyboard glyphs (Tab, Esc, Ctrl/⌘ D, ⌫, 0–9) are NOT translated. They
#      are what is printed on the key.
#
